  [1mProposed design[0m

  - [1mFilter bar[0m above the table, added by JavaScript. With JavaScript off, nothing changes.
    - [1mEvent type:[0m a Select (md) with "All events" and the nine fixed types. Nine options means a Select, per 03-forms.
    - [1mFailed sign-ins only:[0m a quiet button that sets the Event type to "Sign-in failed". The Security note says that's
      the one search people actually came here for, so it gets one click.
    - [1mSearch:[0m a type=search input (lg) that matches device, location and details as you type. It has a clear button,
      Escape clears it, [38;5;153mautocomplete="off"[39m and [38;5;153menterkeyhint="search"[39m. Matching is on text only, with no validation.
    - Visible labels go above the controls, in sentence case.
  - [1mResults count:[0m a polite live region, e.g. "Showing 4 of 48 events". If nothing matches, an EmptyState explains why
    and offers a "Clear filters" button. Changing a filter never moves focus, navigates or submits.
  - [1mTable:[0m I'd add a caption, which the Table spec requires. Filtering just hides rows; the data, order and masked IPs
    stay as they are. "Sign-in failed" rows keep their text label, so color is never the only cue.
  - [1mNot included:[0m a date range filter, since 90 days newest-first is already short, and anything touching the API,
    export or actions.
  - [1mFiles changed:[0m [38;5;153mactivity.html[39m (caption, filter container), [38;5;153mactivity.js[39m (filter logic) and [38;5;153mactivity.css[39m (filter bar
    styles). I expect it to stay well under 300 lines.
  - [1mChecks:[0m a line-by-line pass against guidelines 01–04, then a manual pass in Safari with VoiceOver and Firefox. In
    each browser I'll filter by type, use the failed sign-ins button, search, clear with Escape and the clear button,
    check keyboard focus rings and reflow at 320px, and load with JavaScript off. I'll report exactly what I clicked.

  [1mTwo scope decisions I need from you before I start:[0m

  1. [1mToken sheet.[0m Any new CSS I write must use tokens, and there's nothing to reference yet. My suggestion is a new
     [38;5;153mpublic/tokens.css[39m that declares only the tokens this page uses, with values from 01-foundations, as its own commit.
     If the shared sheet lives somewhere else, for example a separate repo that ops serves, tell me the path and I'll
     link to it instead.
  2. [1mFixes to the existing page.[0m Should I also fix the empty no-JavaScript table, the raw hex/px values in [38;5;153mactivity.css[39m
     and the date format? Each would be its own [38;5;153mfix:[39m commit. The no-JavaScript fix means putting the rows into the HTML
     as well, which duplicates the inline data until the API is wired up. Or should I keep this branch to the filter
     alone and leave those for another branch?

  Is this design approved, and how do you want to handle those two points?

